---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { siteConfig } from '../data/site';

const effectiveDate = 'Effective August 7, 2026';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy policy | Creaturely"
  description="How Creaturely keeps animal health journals local, what leaves the device only by choice, and what Vector42 does not collect."
  heading="Privacy, in plain language."
  intro="Creaturely is designed so you can keep a complete animal health journal without giving that journal to us."
  effectiveDate={effectiveDate}
>
  <nav slot="contents">
    <a href="#scope">Scope</a>
    <a href="#collection">What we collect</a>
    <a href="#local-data">Data on your device</a>
    <a href="#data-exits">When data leaves</a>
    <a href="#recovery">Optional recovery</a>
    <a href="#permissions">Permissions</a>
    <a href="#retention">Retention and deletion</a>
    <a href="#security">Security</a>
    <a href="#changes">Changes and contact</a>
  </nav>

  <section id="scope">
    <h2>Scope</h2>
    <p>
      This policy applies to the Creaturely mobile application for iOS and Android and to this
      public Creaturely website. Creaturely is published by {siteConfig.publisher}.
    </p>
    <p>
      Creaturely is a personal journal. It is not a medical device, veterinary service, or
      diagnostic tool.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="collection">
    <h2>What Vector42 collects</h2>
    <p>
      Vector42 collects no Creaturely journal data. The app has no account of any kind, no
      Vector42 backend, no advertising, no analytics, and no crash-reporting or remote-logging
      SDK.
    </p>
    <p>
      This website uses no analytics, advertising, tracking pixels, account system, or nonessential
      cookies. Azure Static Web Apps and ordinary network infrastructure process technical request
      information, such as an IP address and browser headers, when serving the site. That processing
      is governed by the infrastructure provider and is not used by Creaturely to build profiles or
      measure product behavior.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="local-data">
    <h2>Data kept on your device</h2>
    <p>
      Normal journaling uses local SQLite storage and app-managed files. Depending on what you
      enter, this can include:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>animal profiles, photos, species, breeds, approximate ages, and notes;</li>
      <li>identifiers such as microchips, tags, bands, and tattoos;</li>
      <li>weight entries and manual resting or sleeping breathing sessions;</li>
      <li>medications, schedules, reminders, and dose status;</li>
      <li>allergies, conditions or body issues, and custom observations;</li>
      <li>documents and images that you choose to import; and</li>
      <li>app preferences, units, and notification settings.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Creaturely does not send this information to Vector42 during normal use. Local data remains
      the source of truth even when optional recovery is enabled.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="data-exits">
    <h2>When data leaves your device</h2>
    <p>Data can leave the app only through an action you take or a feature you configure:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        You can send a temporary PDF, CSV, or ZIP through the operating system share sheet. The
        app removes its temporary share copy after the interaction returns.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can save a <code>.creaturely</code> backup, health summary, raw-data export, or document
        package through the system file picker. The destination provider applies its own terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        Camera and photo-library features read only the media you capture, grant, or select, then
        copy it into app-managed storage.
      </li>
      <li>
        Source-code and support links open GitHub in your chosen browser. Creaturely does not add
        journal data to those links.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Think carefully before sharing a health summary or backup. Once another person or service
      receives a copy, their handling of it is outside Creaturely's control.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="recovery">
    <h2>Optional private recovery</h2>
    <p>
      If you opt in, Creaturely can store validated backup snapshots in your own private platform
      storage. iOS uses the private CloudKit database associated with your Apple account. Android
      uses Google Drive's hidden app-data folder with the limited <code>drive.appdata</code> scope.
    </p>
    <p>
      Recovery is backup and restore, not live sync. Creaturely does not receive your cloud account
      credentials or the contents of those snapshots. Apple and Google process the copies under
      their own privacy terms.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="permissions">
    <h2>Device permissions</h2>
    <p>Creaturely asks for access only when a feature needs it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Notifications support medication, breathing-check, and weight reminders.</li>
      <li>Camera and photo access support animal photos and selected records.</li>
      <li>File access supports imports, exports, and backups through system pickers.</li>
      <li>Network access supports optional private cloud recovery and external links.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Denying a permission limits the related feature but does not prevent ordinary local
      journaling.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="retention">
    <h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      You control local journal entries inside the app. Removing an entry deletes it from the live
      journal according to the app's normal safeguards. Uninstalling Creaturely removes its local
      app data according to your operating system's behavior.
    </p>
    <p>
      Exports and backups remain wherever you chose to save or share them. Private recovery
      snapshots remain in your platform account until you remove them through Creaturely or the
      provider's available controls.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="security">
    <h2>Security and backup files</h2>
    <p>
      Creaturely validates backup checksums and relationships before restore and creates a safety
      snapshot before replacing the live journal. These controls help detect corruption and avoid
      partial restores.
    </p>
    <p>
      A <code>.creaturely</code> backup is not encrypted by the app. Protect exported files using a
      trusted destination, device passcode, full-disk encryption, and any security controls offered
      by your storage provider.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="changes">
    <h2>Policy changes and contact</h2>
    <p>
      If Creaturely's data practices change, this policy and the in-repository privacy boundary will
      be updated before the new behavior is released. The effective date at the top will also
      change.
    </p>
    <p>
      Questions can be submitted through the <a href={siteConfig.supportUrl}>Creaturely support form</a>.
      GitHub issues are public. Do not include real animal health information, backups, photos,
      documents, addresses, or other personal information.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalLayout>
